<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bohol</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bohol is an island province in the Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visayas">Visayas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, known for the Chocolate Hills. Before Spanish rule it had coastal communities led by datus, and in 1565 its chief Sikatuna made a blood compact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a>. It was the scene of the long Dagohoy revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the 18th and 19th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bohol.jpg" alt="Bohol" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philip Nalangan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chocolate_Hills_Bohol_Philippines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bohol lies in the Central Visayas, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> to the west across the Cebu Strait, Leyte to the northeast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> to the south across the Bohol Sea. The province consists of the main island and about 70 smaller ones, of which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panglao-island">Panglao Island</a> is the largest. The main island has a land area of 4,821 km2. The terrain is rolling and hilly, about half the island is covered in limestone, and the interior is a large plateau. The name is derived from the local word bo-ol, a kind of tree that flourished on the island. Boholanos share a language with the Cebuano but keep a conscious distinction from them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Southeastern Bohol, especially Cogtong Bay in Candijay and the Anda Peninsula, is called the birthplace of Bohol's civilisation because of petroglyphs and burials in boat-shaped wooden coffins, estimated to be 6,000 years old. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> arrived on 16 March 1565 seeking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and gold. After convincing the datus that his men were not Portuguese, he made a peace pact with Datu Sikatuna, sealed with a sandugo, a blood compact. It is marked each year at the Sandugo Festival and appears on the provincial flag and seal.</p><p>American troops took over peacefully in March 1899, but in September 1900 Colonel Pedro Samson led 2,000 men in rebellion against their harsh treatment. American forces answered with a scorched-earth policy and burned 20 of Bohol's 35 towns. Japanese troops landed at Tagbilaran on 17 May 1942, and local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> resisted until Filipino and American troops landed on 11 April 1945. Carlos P. Garcia, born in Talibon, became president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1957, and Tagbilaran received its city charter on 18 June 1966.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tagbilaran is the capital and largest city of the province, which has one component city, 47 municipalities and 1,109 barangays. Its population was 1,412,726 at the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Most people speak Bohol Cebuano, and the Eskayans of the southeastern interior have an artificial language of their own, with its own alphabet. The Chocolate Hills, mounds of limestone, are the best-known attraction, and the Philippine tarsier, one of the world's smallest primates, is native to the island. Bohol became a UNESCO Global Geopark in 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bohol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was a settlement of the <b>Bohol</b> coast, probably on Panglao Island, ruled by datus and trading with other Visayan and Mindanao ports.</blockquote><a href="/state/kedatuan-of-dapitan">Kedatuan of Dapitan</a></li></ul></section>
